  "question": {
    "text": "A metallic ring of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> and radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> with a uniform metallic spoke of same mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> and length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> is rotated about its axis with angular velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>rev</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>sec</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. In a perpendicular uniform magnetic field&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> of magnitude&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> as shown in the figure. If the central end of the spoke is connected to the rim of the wheel through a resistor&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> of magnitude <math><mi>π</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math> as shown. The resistor does not rotate, it's one end is always at the centre of the ring and another end is always in contact with the ring. A force&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi></math> as shown is needed to maintain the constant angular velocity of the spoke then,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi></math> is equal to (The ring and the spoke has zero resistance)<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0028_fcac14126234.jpg\" />"
  },
